@DaveCTurner Would it possible to bring back the TLS1.2 behaviour with the `jdk.tls.acknowledgeCloseNotify` system property?
https://bugs.openjdk.java.net/browse/JDK-8208526?subTaskView=all
https://stackoverflow.com/questions/54687831/changes-in-sslengine-usage-when-going-up-to-tlsv1-3

I investigated this a bit further and discovered that `io.netty.handler.ssl.SslHandler#sslCloseFuture()` exposes a future which is completed on receipt of a `close_notify` from the peer. Today we don't subscribe to this future, but I believe we should do, so I think that's the bug.

This technically affects inbound and outbound transport connections as well as inbound HTTP connections. We don't really have a notion of gracefully closing a transport connection so I think we could reasonably just close the channel straight away:

```diff
diff --git a/x-pack/plugin/security/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/core/security/transport/netty4/SecurityNetty4Transport.java b/x-pack/plugin/security/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/core/security/transport/netty4/SecurityNetty4Transport.java
index 02533a97e61..cb3393f9503 100644
--- a/x-pack/plugin/security/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/core/security/transport/netty4/SecurityNetty4Transport.java
+++ b/x-pack/plugin/security/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/core/security/transport/netty4/SecurityNetty4Transport.java
@@ -137,6 +137,7 @@ public class SecurityNetty4Transport extends Netty4Transport {
             serverEngine.setUseClientMode(false);
             final SslHandler sslHandler = new SslHandler(serverEngine);
             ch.pipeline().addFirst("sslhandler", sslHandler);
+            sslHandler.sslCloseFuture().addListener(f -> ch.close());
             super.initChannel(ch);
             assert ch.pipeline().first() == sslHandler : "SSL handler must be first handler in pipeline";
         }
@@ -217,7 +218,10 @@ public class SecurityNetty4Transport extends Netty4Transport {
                 sslParameters.setServerNames(Collections.singletonList(serverName));
                 sslEngine.setSSLParameters(sslParameters);
             }
-            ctx.pipeline().replace(this, "ssl", new SslHandler(sslEngine));
+            final SslHandler sslHandler = new SslHandler(sslEngine);
+            ctx.pipeline().replace(this, "ssl", sslHandler);
+            sslHandler.sslCloseFuture().addListener(f -> ctx.close());
+
             super.connect(ctx, remoteAddress, localAddress, promise);
         }
     }
```

I'm in two minds about HTTP channels however. We could integrate with the `HttpPipeliningAggregator` and delay closing our end of the channel until we've sent all the pipelined responses. That seems like the proper thing to do, and is what I suggested in my previous message, but I now worry that this would delay the cancellation that a client close is trying to trigger. I expect most clients do not eagerly send a `close_notify` to indicate the end of a series of pipelined requests for which it's still awaiting responses. It would be technically correct to do so in TLS1.3 but in earlier TLS versions it just wouldn't work.
